{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"`bridge status` becomes the canonical way to check the bridge, and the heartbeat canary consumes it\non a schedule and escalates via tickets rather than being a parallel alarm. The former\n`bridge-status` spelling is restored as a compatibility entrypoint that delegates to the same\ndurable implementation, so environments using the old vocabulary gain the real status behavior\nwithout an immediate command break.\n\nInvestigation found THREE existing indicators, none of them `.bridge-status.json`:\nthe heartbeat canary (the real production channel — hourly, files bug tickets tagged\n`heartbeat-alert`/`binding-drift-alert`); `bridge_state/health/<pass_id>.json` (a complete per-pass\nrecord, written on success AND failure with `failure_kind`, and read by NOBODY); and the tickets\nbranch itself (6,026 `chore: sync events from rebar reconciler [run <id>]` commits).\n\n**The trap that must not be walked into:** there are TWO `bridge_state` directories.\n`bridge_state/` (no dot) is GITIGNORED and never uploaded as an artifact — in production it is\nwritten on an ephemeral runner and destroyed with it. `.tickets-tracker/.bridge_state/` is\ncommitted. A status verb reading `health/` or `bridge_alerts/` would work perfectly for whoever\nbuilds it and report NOTHING REAL in CI. On a dev checkout those files are five weeks stale while\nthe bridge ran through Aug 4.\n\n**And the commit-back step has no `if:` condition** (`reconcile-bridge.yml:272`), so GHA skips it\nwhenever the reconciler step fails. A committed-only record would durably capture every success and\nsilently drop every hard failure — inverted.\n\nDesign: a ref-blob `refs/reconciler/last-pass` carries the minimal always-pushed truth (written by\nthe reconciler process itself, not the workflow), and a committed file carries rich detail. Crashed\nvs running is answered by `refs/reconciler/lock`'s FENCE (a progress witness observed across one\nlease) — never by comparing `heartbeat_ns` to the local clock, which reintroduces the clock-skew bug\nthe lease design eliminated.\n\nCross-environment safety: stamp the writer env and have status report only matching records, with a\ndistinct FOREIGN verdict rather than silently falling back. Do NOT use `.env-id` — it is gitignored\nand regenerated per run, so an ephemeral runner gets a new one every pass. Use the configured\n`REBAR_ENV_ID`. Only mutating passes write the durable record.\n\nConsolidate on the way in: `health.record_pass` and `pass_io._write_pass_record` are already TWO\nproducers of this same fact, and the latter's docstring says it should commit to the tickets branch.\nDo not create a third. Drop the dead `pre_fsck`/`post_fsck` fields and `capture_baseline`.\n\nSalvage note corrected: `count_unresolved_alerts()` is NOT salvageable — zero `BRIDGE_ALERT` files\nexist across all 39,160 files on `origin/tickets`, so it is provably always 0.\n\n\n## Vocabulary compatibility\n\nCurrent help, workflows, canary code, and docs use `rebar bridge status`. The old\n`rebar bridge-status` spelling remains executable but routes to the identical status parser/core,\nincluding `--max-age`, structured output, exit behavior, environment selection, and paused\nhandling. Tests run both spellings against the same durable refs and assert equal results and no\nstate mutation. Release notes supersede the earlier tombstone-removal note with the restored\ncompatibility mapping.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `bridge status` reports last run, in-flight state, and what needs attention, reading only DURABLE state\n- [ ] Legacy `bridge-status` remains executable and produces the same durable result, structured output, options, and exit behavior as `bridge status`; current help/docs/canary use the new spelling\n- [ ] A local run never reports a developer's own pass as production's\n- [ ] Crashed is distinguished from running via the lock's fence across one lease, not a clock comparison\n- [ ] A hard failure is still recorded despite the skipped commit-back step\n- [ ] `--max-age` exits non-zero on staleness; `status` exits 0 while merely paused\n- [ ] The canary calls `status` and stops querying the GitHub Actions API for the heartbeat\n- [ ] Exactly one producer of the pass record remains"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786215269502623001,"uuid":"6e0169c3-ebb6-4b89-80ea-f279ec9a81b5"}